To:       whoever keeps the world
Subject:  [PATCH] say it the customary way

The greeting is by custom given with a comma in the middle and an
exclamation mark on the end. The program gives it with neither. I took
this for an oversight, of the kind that gets through when a thing is
five lines long and nobody thinks to read it twice.

The change is to one line. The two marks make the line two characters
longer. I took a space off the front to pay for one of them. It was
only sea, and at the edge, where there is plenty. The other sticks out
a little on the east side, where I do not think it will be noticed.

    patch < comma.patch
    make world

I have tried it. It builds without complaint and says the whole thing,
with the pause and the raised voice, and then stops as before.

Not signed. I was only passing.

--- a/world.c
+++ b/world.c
@@ -48,5 +48,5 @@
     ?-y:y)>.935?"*++"[k] :sin(a*2+y*3)+
       cos(a-y*2+4)>.8?"#o:"[k]:"~-."[k
         ] ) ;} } puts ( "" ); puts (
-           "hello world") ;return
+          "hello, world!") ;return
                     0;}

Disposition: not applied.

It was read, which is more than most post here can say, and it does
what it claims. It is declined on four counts and kept on a fifth.

1. The comma is spoken for. It hangs low among the fixed stars, and it
   is also the coin, of which there is one. A second comma in the
   greeting would be a second coin, and the currency is sound only
   because it cannot be added to. The patch is, in the strict sense,
   a forgery, though a civil one.

2. The exclamation mark is not available either. It goes round a long
   way out and comes past every seventy-six minutes. It has been asked
   down before and did not answer. A copy of it set at the end of the
   line would be seen from up there, and it is not known how that
   would be taken.

3. There is no sea at the edge that is not doing anything. The space
   removed was the eleventh of eleven on that line and had held its
   place since the source was put into shape. With it gone the bottom
   of the world leans a character to the west and stands a character
   proud to the east, where it was noticed. Nobody lives there, but
   it is visible from the pole.

4. The greeting has got this far without the pause and without raising
   its voice. It was asked whether it wanted them. It said hello world.

5. It is kept all the same, as the only change anybody has ever
   proposed to the two words, and because the sender went to the
   trouble of counting the spaces. Any visitor may apply it at home.
   The world will then say it the customary way, on that machine and
   no other, and lean slightly, and nothing further will be said about
   it from here.

To put it back:

    patch -R < comma.patch

The space returns to where it was. It does not appear to have minded.
